<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Catalog>
    <!-- The player character's damage. Split out from the weapon because SC2
         keeps "how hard it hits" in the effect and "how often" in the weapon;
         the two together are what P0 tunes when asking whether a player clears
         debris meaningfully faster than a worker does. -->
    <CEffectDamage id="Lob_Hero_Rifle" parent="DU_WEAP">
        <Kind value="Ranged"/>
        <Amount value="20"/>
    </CEffectDamage>

    <CEffectApplyBehavior id="Lob_Debug_Haste">
        <Behavior value="Lob_Debug_Haste"/>
    </CEffectApplyBehavior>
    <!-- Deliberately empty. An ability has to point at an effect; this one's
         whole job is to be pressable so a trigger can hear it. -->
    <CEffectSet id="Lob_Nothing"/>
    <!-- What the work ability fires.

         Apply-behavior, which is Larva Swarm's shape; Snipe uses damage. Either
         is a real effect on a real unit, and that is the part that matters: an
         Effect-Target ability whose effect does not unambiguously act on a unit
         never becomes a targeting command, so pressing the button does nothing
         and no cursor appears.

         Two earlier attempts both had that failure. A CEffectSet with no members
         points at nothing. A CEffectModifyUnit with no fields modifies nothing,
         which turns out not to be the same as acting on something.

         No validator here any more either. IsContainmentGate has a WhichUnit
         field it does not set, so it was very likely testing the caster — an
         employee, which is not a Structure — and failing, which makes the
         ability unusable rather than the target illegal. TargetFilters does the
         target check, and 14_work refuses anything that is not a door anyway.
         It can come back once this works, with WhichUnit set to Target. -->
    <CEffectApplyBehavior id="Lob_WorkCheck">
        <Behavior value="Lob_Worked"/>
    </CEffectApplyBehavior>
</Catalog>
